<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simhavishnu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 575–600</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Avanisimha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simhavishnu was a king of the Pallava dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the late 6th century. He expelled the Kalabhras from the Kaveri delta, taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country, and with it the Pallavas became the leading power of the Tamil lands. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> continued the rise of the dynasty and began its rock-cut temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simhavishnu.jpg" alt="Simhavishnu" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simhavishnu_portrait_in_stone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Simhavishnu, son of Simhavarman III, came to the throne as the Pallava dynasty was beginning to reassert its supremacy. The southern peninsula was then divided among the Pallavas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> in the Tamil and Andhra lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. He was the first Pallava monarch whose domain reached south beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>, and he is credited with making that city a powerful capital.</p><p>After overthrowing the Kalabhras and conquering the land up to the Kaveri River, he came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. The struggle between the Pallavas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, which lasted more than two centuries, also began in his reign. The Pallava influence later seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, in art and in inscriptions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grantha-script">Grantha script</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, is often traced to the maritime foundations of this period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Simhavishnu was a patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharavi">Bharavi</a>, author of the epic Kiratarjuniya on the duel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, whose structure suggests it was meant for Koodiyattam performances at temple festivals. The Udayendiram copper plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a> show him to have been a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>, whereas his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a> was first a Jain and later turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>. Mahendravarman's drama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattavilasa-prahasana">Mattavilasa Prahasana</a> portrays Simhavishnu as a great conqueror, and a portrait of him survives among the stone carvings of the Varaha Cave Temple, the Adivaraha Mandapam, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mahabalipuram</a>, now part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simhavishnu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It became a major state only when <b>Simhavishnu</b>, in the late sixth century, defeated the Kalabhras and extended Pallava rule south to the Kaveri delta, beginning the line usually called the later Pallavas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 6th century the Pandya king Kadungon and the Pallava king <b>Simhavishnu</b> expelled the Kalabhras, founding the First Pandyan Empire and expanding the Pallava dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalabhra-dynasty">Kalabhra dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
